WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.687
A primary school sends a lot.

00:00:01.687 --> 00:00:08.143
Newsletters, letters, calendar changes and class emails, plus whatever turns up in the parents' WhatsApp group.

00:00:08.143 --> 00:00:10.760
Term Time reads all of it, so I can just ask.

00:00:11.363 --> 00:00:12.398
When is half term?

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:17.403
It checks today's date, then looks the term dates up in the school's own calendar feed.

00:00:21.478 --> 00:00:23.154
Every answer carries its source.

00:00:23.154 --> 00:00:27.238
That link opens the calendar entry it came from, so I can check it in one tap.

00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:31.207
With a child in Year 6, I tick Year 6 and ask about the next trip.

00:00:31.207 --> 00:00:35.799
The years I pick go with every question, so the search looks at that year's letters first.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.496
Now Year 6's own letters come first.

00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:49.890
The residential in Kent, the times, what to pack, and a link to the letter that says so.

00:00:52.053 --> 00:00:54.296
Some of it never comes from the school at all.

00:00:54.296 --> 00:00:59.173
Open evenings arrive in the parents' WhatsApp group, so the admin console has a Paste a note screen.

00:00:59.776 --> 00:01:02.296
I paste the message as it arrived, and save it.

00:01:04.222 --> 00:01:11.155
Term Time reads the dates out of the text and fetches any links, so a school's own page can fill in the time and the booking address.

00:01:11.155 --> 00:01:17.410
It is the same extraction that reads the newsletters, and it has to copy dates and links exactly rather than guess them.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.902
A note is public straight away, because pasting it is the decision.

00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:26.185
Here's the open evening it found, with its time, and narrowed to Year 6.

00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:28.551
A photographed letter works too.

00:01:28.551 --> 00:01:33.068
It's read into text I can check before saving, and the photo itself is never kept.

00:01:35.064 --> 00:01:36.511
School email is different.

00:01:36.511 --> 00:01:43.024
It's read every thirty minutes, but it starts out restricted, and nothing reaches the public chat until I approve it.

00:01:43.627 --> 00:01:44.787
Here's how it fits together.

00:01:45.403 --> 00:01:51.803
Four sources: the website and its PDFs, the calendar feed, the school mailbox, and those notes.

00:01:52.405 --> 00:01:57.965
Each one is fetched, its dates and year groups are pulled out, and email is held for approval.

00:01:58.568 --> 00:02:03.048
And the chat answers from what is stored, using tools that look dates up rather than guessing.

00:02:04.355 --> 00:02:08.955
That was Term Time, a school assistant that answers from what the school actually published.

00:02:08.955 --> 00:02:12.355
Every answer carries its source, so parents can check it in one tap.
